Z||000007||StuGood||12-07-2000||11:54 PM||||I always say this when people ask about 4-cylinder to V6 Fiero swaps  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">, but remember you need to change out the fuel pump (drop the tank), if you want to go from stock 4-cyl (TBI) Fiero to a modern V6 (MFI or SFI) engine.<P>Sorry if you already thought of this  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||tgowens||12-09-2000||02:59 AM||||there are quite a few things to change when going from a 2.5L 4-cyl to a 2.8L or 3.4L 6-cyl (we are just completing a 2.5L to 3.4L swap here at the shop now. first of all, yes you can use the 4-speed tranny. In addition to the fuel pump (already mentioned) you also need to utilize the wiring harness and ECM from the 6-cyl donor car. there are other things to consider such as the coolant line which runs to the rear firewall needs to be cut and re-routed to the front side of the engine. The throttle cable will more thn likely need to be replaced as well as the cruise control lines mounted on the rear firewall and possibly the cruise control cable itself. When going to a 3.4 push-rod engine, the starter needs to be mounted on the opposite side of the block which will require redrilling. Personally, I think the easiest swaps are the 3.4L from the '93 - '95 Firebird and Camaro and the 4.9L from the Cadillac (if you have an automatic tranny) If you are going to replace a 2.5L 4-cyl the easiest would be the 3.4L (why drop in a 2.8L when you can drop in a 3.4L with the same effort?) -- just my 3 cents........||||reg||1
Z||000009||bubbajoexx||12-09-2000||10:20 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by tgowens:<BR><B>there are quite a few things to change when going from a 2.5L 4-cyl to a 2.8L or 3.4L 6-cyl (we are just completing a 2.5L to 3.4L swap here at the shop now. first of all, yes you can use the 4-speed tranny. In addition to the fuel pump (already mentioned) you also need to utilize the wiring harness and ECM from the 6-cyl donor car. there are other things to consider such as the coolant line which runs to the rear firewall needs to be cut and re-routed to the front side of the engine. The throttle cable will more thn likely need to be replaced as well as the cruise control lines mounted on the rear firewall and possibly the cruise control cable itself. When going to a 3.4 push-rod engine, the starter needs to be mounted on the opposite side of the block which will require redrilling. Personally, I think the easiest swaps are the 3.4L from the '93 - '95 Firebird and Camaro and the 4.9L from the Cadillac (if you have an automatic tranny) If you are going to replace a 2.5L 4-cyl the easiest would be the 3.4L (why drop in a 2.8L when you can drop in a 3.4L with the same effort?) -- just my 3 cents........</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>the 3.4 from a lumina Z34 is a better chose of engine than that of the Fbody cars it is all ready a front drive engine the fbodys are not.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Oreif||12-09-2000||10:27 AM||||The 3.4 is in a lumina, Grand Am, or F-body, Doesn't matter. Starter is still on the other side of the engine. The blocks are identical.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 12-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||bubbajoexx||12-09-2000||10:30 AM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by RiceBurner515:<BR><B>okay, i know there is hours of reading about engine swaps and so forth, but sorry, i have no time for that. All i need to know is what engines will bolt right up to my 85 SE 4cyl. 5-spd tranny WITHOUT ANY major mods to anything in the engine or compartment, the easiest and will fit the engine mounts? i want a V6 or V8. what is the cheapest and what car will i find the engines in? But i don't want a 2.8, i want 3.0-3.4, i also heard of 4L's swap (4.9, 4.8, 4.7, etc.) Thanks SO MUCH!!! <BR>so~&gt; cheap, easy, no mods to car.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> use the 3.8 from a 85 buick century Ttype and the harness this engine needs no mounts to modify because it uses the same lowwer front mount as the fiero all you need to do is bolt it in rout the exhaust and install the computer and harness from the buick ie (engine harness) and you off thes cars are cheep to buy 150 dollars almost any where .<BR>I done about 20 of these conversions(for about 1500.00 including engine and harness) and the harness is quit easy to install they are the first year multiport fuely front drive buicks and the harness is real simple TO convert with the use of a buick manual such as Hanes.you want cheep thats the way to go<P><BR>[This message has been edited by bubbajoexx (edited 12-09-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by bubbajoexx (edited 12-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||bubbajoexx||12-09-2000||10:44 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B>The 3.4 is in a lumina, Grand Am, or F-body, Doesn't matter. Starter is still on the other side of the engine. The blocks are identical.<P>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 12-09-2000).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>NOT SO the front drive cars have the starter on the same side as the fireo i don't now where you get your info  front drives all have the starter on the left bank because the trans is on the right bank. PLEASE DON'T TRY TO CONFUSE PEOPLE FRONT DRIVES AND REAR DRIVES ARE OPPOSITE TO EACH OTHER.<p>[This message has been edited by bubbajoexx (edited 12-09-2000).]||||reg||8
Z||000013||Eric_PA||12-09-2000||10:54 AM||||There are some front wheel drive cars with the starter on the oposite side from where the starter is located in the Fiero. I know for a fact that my automatic 2.0 OHC engine powered Sunbird is like that. I was going to put the engine in my 84 until I noticed that problem. I would have had to swap the auto out with the engine and didn't want to go from stick to auto.||||reg||1
Z||000014||bubbajoexx||12-09-2000||11:15 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Eric_PA:<BR><B>There are some front wheel drive cars with the starter on the oposite side from where the starter is located in the Fiero. I know for a fact that my automatic 2.0 OHC engine powered Sunbird is like that. I was going to put the engine in my 84 until I noticed that problem. I would have had to swap the auto out with the engine and didn't want to go from stick to auto.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>That is the excption but also the 2.0 OHC wont bolt up to a fiero transaxle either . you have to use engine and trans from the sunbird to install it in a fiero and why would you any way the 2.0 is the worst engine pontiac ever built they are nutorious for bad head gasket failure and camshaft oil failure. also dont make any sence to put such a failue prone  engine in a great car as the fiero even the turbo version is junk sorry but it is true I have to change one that failed in a 94 sunbird on monday with only 64000 klm on it. <p>[This message has been edited by bubbajoexx (edited 12-09-2000).]||||reg||9
Z||000015||PlasticWrapd||12-09-2000||01:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by RiceBurner515:<BR><B>okay, i know there is hours of reading about engine swaps and so forth, but sorry, i have no time for that... </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Unfortunately, the reading and research part is just as important, if not more so, as the swap itself.  I have performed a few swaps and I'll never forget my first one.  It was a real learning experience as I did not do the required research before hand.  It ended up taking three months when I figured on it taking about four days!  If you're not prepared, then be ready for a total nightmare!||||reg||4
